1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,909
What is a pull request?

2
00:00:07,467 --> 00:00:15,131
A pull request (PR) is a request to merge one branch into another, wrapped in a page for discussion:

3
00:00:15,131 --> 00:00:20,266
the commits, the diff, a merge check, reviews and automated checks.

4
00:00:20,266 --> 00:00:24,941
Underneath it is plain Git, which we reproduce locally first.

5
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:31,100
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

6
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:36,442
What a PR from feature-tea into main would show, computed locally.

7
00:00:36,442 --> 00:00:37,316
Commits tab:

8
00:00:37,316 --> 00:00:40,740
commits on the branch that are not on the base:

9
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:42,051
Files changed tab:

10
00:00:42,051 --> 00:00:43,800
the three-dot diff, i.e.

11
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:51,668
the branch's changes since it left main (main's own new commit, "Add opening hours", is not part of the PR):

12
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:53,444
Files changed tab:

13
00:00:53,444 --> 00:00:55,059
the three-dot diff, i.e.

14
00:00:55,059 --> 00:01:02,323
the branch's changes since it left main (main's own new commit, "Add opening hours", is not part of the PR):

15
00:01:02,323 --> 00:01:06,763
Merge check ("This branch has no conflicts with the base branch"):

16
00:01:06,763 --> 00:01:09,723
a merge computed without touching any files:

17
00:01:10,267 --> 00:01:14,607
Merge check ("This branch has no conflicts with the base branch"):

18
00:01:14,607 --> 00:01:17,501
a merge computed without touching any files:

19
00:01:19,267 --> 00:01:20,788
Let me try something.

20
00:01:20,788 --> 00:01:28,901
A branch that conflicts with its base, like a PR GitHub marks "This branch has conflicts that must be resolved":

21
00:01:29,467 --> 00:01:32,307
Exit status 1 and CONFLICT (content):

22
00:01:32,307 --> 00:01:34,534
Merge conflict in prices.txt:

23
00:01:34,534 --> 00:01:37,298
both branches changed the same line.

24
00:01:37,298 --> 00:01:42,211
GitHub cannot merge the PR; the author has to update the branch.

25
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:50,832
The PR author brings main into the branch and resolves the conflict there (lesson 27),

26
00:01:50,832 --> 00:01:53,626
then pushes; the PR updates itself:

27
00:01:55,367 --> 00:01:56,080
Got it.

28
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,831
What do I need to remember?

29
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:05,026
A PR = a branch comparison + discussion + checks + a merge button.

30
00:02:05,567 --> 00:02:14,226
Commits tab = base..branch; Files changed = base...branch; conflicts = git merge-tree.

31
00:02:14,767 --> 00:02:21,631
Conflicts are fixed on the branch by its author; the PR updates automatically when the branch is pushed.

32
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:27,125
I keep hearing about creating a pull request.

33
00:02:27,125 --> 00:02:28,711
What is it, really?

34
00:02:29,267 --> 00:02:31,934
The full loop on the real GitHub:

35
00:02:31,934 --> 00:02:39,775
branch, commit, push, open a PR with a useful title and description, update it with more commits.

36
00:02:39,775 --> 00:02:44,221
We use the GitHub CLI; the web flow is shown alongside.

37
00:02:45,967 --> 00:02:50,366
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

38
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:55,016
GitHub even prints the link to create the PR.

39
00:02:55,016 --> 00:02:56,205
With the CLI:

40
00:02:56,767 --> 00:03:00,882
GitHub even prints the link to create the PR.

41
00:03:00,882 --> 00:03:02,071
With the CLI:

42
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:04,233
Let's run it.

43
00:03:05,967 --> 00:03:07,865
Let me try something.

44
00:03:07,865 --> 00:03:11,751
Run gh pr create again for the same branch:

45
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:16,235
A branch can have only one open PR into the same base.

46
00:03:16,235 --> 00:03:18,422
The second one is unnecessary:

47
00:03:18,422 --> 00:03:22,357
pushing to the branch already updated the existing PR.

48
00:03:22,357 --> 00:03:28,625
If the title or description needs to change, edit the PR instead of opening a new one.

49
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:32,166
Here is the fix.

50
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:34,613
Got it.

51
00:03:34,613 --> 00:03:37,365
What do I need to remember?

52
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:44,245
Push the branch, then gh pr create (or the "Compare & pull request" button).

53
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:50,495
New commits on the branch update the PR; one open PR per branch and base.

54
00:03:51,033 --> 00:03:57,158
Write the why, what, how-tested in the description; drafts for early feedback.

55
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:01,615
How does pull request review work?

56
00:04:02,167 --> 00:04:04,379
How a review works on GitHub:

57
00:04:04,379 --> 00:04:10,938
general comments, comments on a line, suggestions the author can apply with one click,

58
00:04:10,938 --> 00:04:15,972
and the three review verdicts (comment, approve, request changes).

59
00:04:15,972 --> 00:04:18,946
And why you cannot approve your own PR.

60
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:25,100
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

61
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:29,140
A general review comment (verdict "Comment"):

62
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:33,348
A comment on a specific line, containing a suggestion.

63
00:04:33,348 --> 00:04:38,959
The CLI has no command for line comments, so we use the API (on the web:

64
00:04:38,959 --> 00:04:42,388
hover the line in "Files changed", click +):

65
00:04:42,933 --> 00:04:47,215
A comment on a specific line, containing a suggestion.

